    "q-158540": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158540",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3f(x+2)?$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=3f(x+2),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $2$ unit to the left and get the graph of \n$y=f(x+2).$  After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-2,1)=(-3,1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the y-axis and get the graph of \n$y=3f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3,3\\cdot 1)=(-3,3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-1)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3f(x+2).$ Substituting $x=-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3f(-3+2) \\\\  & =3f(-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-3,3)$ must lie on $y=3f(x+2).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
