    "q-121421": {
      "dom_id": "step-q121421",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "The point  $(-2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+5?$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-2,-1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+5:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2+1,-1)=(-1,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(-1))=(-1,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,1+5)=(-1,6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-2,-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-2+1)=-1\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=-1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+5 \\\\  & =-(-1)+5 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,6)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
